const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Physician Training Content Development","serviceType":"Clinical education content development for accredited providers, hospitals and medical societies","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/physician-writing-services/physician-training/","description":"Clinical education content written for an accredited provider to certify: objectives first, assessment blueprinted, guidelines cited by version.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Can Pubrica accredit our CME activity?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. We are a content developer and not an accredited provider, and no content developer can accredit anything. In the United States, to certify an activity for AMA PRA Category 1 Credit the sponsoring organisation must be accredited by the ACCME or by a recognised state medical society — and even then the division of authority is worth knowing: the ACCME accredits providers, while the AMA owns the credit, and the ACCME’s own glossary states that the designation of credit is not within its purview. So “ACCME-accredited CME credit” is a phrase that describes nothing. What a developer can properly do is work under joint providership — the ACCME’s own term for an activity planned, implemented and evaluated by an accredited provider together with one or more non-accredited organisations. The accredited provider certifies, designates and remains fully responsible for compliance. We write. Note also that “education partner” is not ACCME terminology and does not appear in its glossary, whatever it appears in on other websites."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do you deliver the training, or only write it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Only write it. Watch for pages that disclaim “direct training services” and then list instructor-led sessions and workshops as formats a few lines later, because one of the two statements is not true. To be unambiguous: we develop content that your faculty, your provider or your platform delivers. We do not run sessions and we do not supply faculty. What we do build, where you want it, is the material that lets somebody else teach it well — a facilitator guide with what to say, what to ask, where the discussion usually stalls and how to restart it, and timings that assume the session will overrun. That is the difference between a deck only its author can teach from and one a department can use for three years."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"We are a pharmaceutical company. What can we actually do?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Less than most companies expect, and the position differs sharply between the United States and Europe. Under the ACCME Standards for Integrity and Independence an ineligible company is one whose primary business is producing, marketing, selling, re-selling or distributing healthcare products used by or on patients — which covers pharmaceutical, device and diagnostics companies. An ineligible company cannot be an accredited provider and cannot control the content of accredited education. The route that remains is commercial support: you fund the activity, the accredited provider controls the content independently, and the arrangement is managed under Standard 4. In Europe the EACCME states its position directly: it will not consider for accreditation events where the content, format or faculty is influenced by industry, submitted by industry, or where industry is the CME provider. Applications come from physician organisations only. A company may co-develop with one; it cannot apply. We will tell you which of these applies to your activity before quoting."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How many CME credits will our activity carry?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"That is the accredited provider’s decision, not ours and not yours. The usual basis is one credit per hour of participation, and in the United Kingdom the Federation of the Royal Colleges of Physicians is explicit about its version: one credit for each hour of CPD provided, capped at eight credits per day, with hours rounded to the nearest whole number. The European system awards ECMEC credits through the EACCME, which the UEMS states are recognised in Europe, the United States and Canada. In India credit is granted by state medical councils and the requirements differ from state to state, with no single national scheme — so the answer depends on where your learners are registered. What a developer can do is build the content to the contact hours you specify and hand the provider the documentation they need; the number they designate is theirs to set."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What makes a learning objective a good one?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"That something can observe whether it happened. “Understand the pathophysiology” and “be aware of current guidelines” are topic headings wearing objective clothing: nothing observes understanding or awareness, so nothing assesses them, and an objective nothing assesses cannot tell you whether the session worked. Rewrite them into behaviours — identify the three features that distinguish this from its commonest mimics; state the threshold at which the guideline recommends escalation and name the guideline; escalate within the timeframe the local protocol specifies. Then map each one to at least two assessment points, so a post-test score can be read per objective rather than only as a total. Two practical rules: write the objectives before any content exists, because one written afterwards describes whatever the deck happens to contain; and hold a one-hour session to four or five objectives, because nine is a list of what the author knows about the subject."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do we know the training actually changed anything?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Not from a satisfaction form, which measures whether people enjoyed it. Three things get you closer. Map the evaluation to the objectives so the data says which objective failed rather than that satisfaction was 4.2 — that alone changes how a programme improves between runs. Use a pre- and post-test with items tagged to objectives, and report the change per objective. And, where the education came from a measurable gap, re-measure the gap: if the audit showed escalation outside the protocol timeframe in a third of cases, re-audit at six months. That third measure is the only one that answers the question you actually asked, and it is the one that almost never gets built in because it has to be planned before the training runs rather than after. We build the instruments; the re-audit is yours to run."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What formats do you deliver content in?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Slide decks with speaker notes, e-learning modules packaged as SCORM or xAPI and tested on your platform, clinical case sets and simulation scenarios, facilitator guides, participant workbooks, quick-reference handouts, assessment banks, video scripts and storyboards, and print-ready manuals. Always as source files rather than exports, so you can amend them next year without commissioning them again. One thing worth separating: packaging content as SCORM is a delivery decision, not an accreditation one. The two are regularly conflated, and an LMS-ready module is not thereby accredited — accreditation attaches to a provider and credit to an activity, however the content is packaged."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Our guidelines change every year. Does the content have to be rebuilt?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No, if it was built to be edited. That is why we hand over source files rather than exports, cite every guideline with its version and date, and keep the clinical claims traceable to a reference list somebody can check in an afternoon. A refresh then amends the slides the change touches and leaves the rest alone. It is also why we tell you at handover where a guideline is due for revision within the life of the programme, so that the content can be structured around the volatile parts rather than through them. An annual guideline refresh is quoted separately and is a fraction of the original build. What does require a rebuild is content delivered as flattened exports with undated references, which is unfortunately most of what departments inherit."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Physician writing","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/physician-writing-services/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Physician training","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/physician-writing-services/physician-training/"}]}];

